WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.467
Lists.

2
00:00:01.767 --> 00:00:04.224
The list is Python's workhorse container.

3
00:00:04.224 --> 00:00:07.400
Master three moves and you can shape almost any data.

4
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:12.133
A list is ordered and mutable, and it can hold anything at all.

5
00:00:12.433 --> 00:00:16.167
Fluency comes down to indexing, slicing, and comprehensions.

6
00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:19.667
Plus knowing which operations are cheap and which are not.

7
00:00:20.867 --> 00:00:25.533
Every element has two addresses, one counting from the front and one from the back.

8
00:00:25.833 --> 00:00:27.933
Index zero is the first item.

9
00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:31.167
Negative one is the last, no length math needed.

10
00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:32.996
A slice takes a range.

11
00:00:32.996 --> 00:00:38.000
Start is included, stop is excluded, so one to four returns three items.

12
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.600
Comprehensions replace a four line loop.

13
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:44.233
We pair each asteroid with its diameter.

14
00:00:44.533 --> 00:00:48.433
We keep only the pairs where the diameter is above zero point four.

15
00:00:48.733 --> 00:00:50.167
We pull out just the name.

16
00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:52.167
And out comes a brand new list.

17
00:00:53.367 --> 00:00:55.133
Here's all three moves in code.

18
00:00:55.433 --> 00:00:58.767
Index zero and negative one give the first and last.

19
00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:04.026
A slice takes the middle, and colon colon negative one makes a reversed copy.

20
00:01:04.026 --> 00:01:05.700
The original is untouched.

21
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.033
And the comprehension keeps only the asteroids wider than point four kilometers.

22
00:01:12.233 --> 00:01:14.767
One performance rule is worth remembering.

23
00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:17.167
Append adds at the end, and it's cheap.

24
00:01:17.467 --> 00:01:22.233
Inserting at the front shifts every other element, so it gets slower as the list grows.

25
00:01:22.533 --> 00:01:25.433
For a queue, reach for collections dot deque instead.

26
00:01:26.633 --> 00:01:29.433
In a RAG system, this is every single query.

27
00:01:29.733 --> 00:01:34.267
The retriever returns fifty candidate chunks, already sorted by the reranker.

28
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:39.267
One comprehension keeps only the chunks scoring above zero point three five.

29
00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:42.433
One slice caps it at eight to fit the prompt budget.

30
00:01:43.633 --> 00:01:44.422
Let's recap.

31
00:01:44.422 --> 00:01:45.867
Index from either end.

32
00:01:46.167 --> 00:01:48.033
Slices clip instead of crashing.

33
00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:51.100
Comprehensions filter and reshape in one line.

34
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.533
And avoid inserting at the front.

35
00:01:53.533 --> 00:01:54.567
Next up: tuples.
